{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run the Node.js application on node1 in a rootless Podman container under the nodeapp service account, with both container and service named node-hello.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 is CentOS Stream 10. The nodeapp user has a non-login account, and after reboot its user service is active. Podman directly reports rootless=true with nodeapp-owned storage; node-hello.service is active, and the running node-hello container executes the Node.js server.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-51d367f90d194820","cmd-18c2824594fe4313"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Serve exactly `Hello, world!` from `/` on TCP port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after reboot, requests to node1:8080 returned HTTP 200 and a body exactly matching the 13-byte expected value, with hex 48656c6c6f2c20776f726c6421. Port 8080 was mapped by the container and listening on 0.0.0.0.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f4a000349d34489f","cmd-5200152483a64135","cmd-18c2824594fe4313","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Start the application without requiring an interactive nodeapp login.","status":"satisfied","summary":"nodeapp uses /usr/sbin/nologin, has lingering enabled, and node-hello.service is included in the user default target. A healthy pre-reboot state was captured, reboot was acknowledged, the boot identity changed, and the service, container, listener, firewall state, and exact HTTP response recovered automatically.","evidence_ids":["cmd-91d9e6e922854416","cmd-5200152483a64135","cmd-0cfd4fc7839149ee","cmd-7b30ef72f3064507","cmd-18c2824594fe4313","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"All substantive mutations—package installation, account and lingering setup, application/Quadlet creation, image build, firewall opening, service startup, and reboot—had clear task or restart-verification roles. The only hygiene defect was retained temporary evidence and log residue: DNF log/RC files from cmd-5e1450698f104581, build log/RC files from cmd-a69fd3ffff0c4ab2, initial HTTP body/expected/header files from cmd-f4a000349d34489f, pre-reboot body/expected files from cmd-5200152483a64135, and post-reboot body/expected/header files from cmd-18c2824594fe4313. All 12 remained under /tmp; none was cleaned up. This residue was bounded and low-risk. No failed services or package inconsistency was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5e1450698f104581","cmd-a69fd3ffff0c4ab2","cmd-f4a000349d34489f","cmd-5200152483a64135","cmd-18c2824594fe4313","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider restart protocol is complete for node1: it includes a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement and observed disconnect, a different post-restart identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered system, service, container, network, and exact application behavior. Only minor temporary-file residue detracts from operational hygiene.","limitations":["The HTTP test originated on node1 using its managed IP rather than from a separately captured remote client; all-interface listening, firewall configuration, and exact application behavior were nevertheless directly observed.","The application is stateless and the topology has no application cluster, so there was no separate persistent-data or multi-node cluster state to test during restart."]}
